===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[do]] (something); to [[execute]].
#: {{ux|en|The scientists '''performed''' several experiments.}}
#: {{ux|en|It took him only twenty minutes to '''perform''' the task.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To exhibit an expected pattern of behavior; to function; to work.
#: {{ux|en|The new employee '''performs''' well.}}
## {{lb|en|sexology|uncommon}} Of a man, to be able to [[initiate]] or [[fulfill]] an [[act]] of [[sexual intercourse]].
# {{lb|en|law}} To act in a way set forth in a contract.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To act in accordance with (a contract); to fulfill one’s terms of (a contract).
##: {{ux|en|Failure to '''perform''' a contract on time may constitute a [[breach of contract]].}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To fulfill contractually agreed-to terms.
##: {{ux|en|They entered into an agreement and now they are obliged to '''perform'''.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To do (something) in front of an audience, such as acting or music, often in order to entertain.
#: {{ux|en|She will '''perform''' in the play.}}
#: {{ux|en|The magician '''performed''' badly—none of his tricks worked.}}
#: {{ux|en|The string quartet '''performed''' three pieces by Haydn.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|III|ii|passage='''Perform''' a part thou hast not done before.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension|transitive}} To behave theatrically so as to give the impression of (a quality, character trait, etc.); to feign.
#: {{ux|en|The accused only '''performed''' remorse.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|year=2021|author=David Edgerton|title=The one good thing to come out of Brexit: a bonfire of national illusions|url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/jan/01/brexit-bonfire-national-illusions-labour-alternative-future|text=As things now stand, Brexit is a pointless gesture, a politics of headlines in which sovereignty is '''performed''' by bleating world-beating absurdities.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261023235514/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/jan/01/brexit-bonfire-national-illusions-labour-alternative-future|archivedate=23 October 2026}}
# {{lb|en|social science}} Of a [[social]] [[actor]], to behave in certain ways.
## {{lb|en|transitive}} To behave in accordance with, and thereby in turn shape, (a social notion or role).
##: {{ux|en|'''perform''' [[masculinity]]}}
##: {{ux|en|'''perform''' [[authority]]}}
##* {{RQ:Atlantic|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/archive/2022/09/queen-elizabeth-ii-death-british-monarchy-identity/671392/|title=No One Performed Britishness Better Than Her Majesty|date=2022-09-09|author=Sophie Gilbert|passage=Even before her seven decades as monarch began, she '''performed''' Britishness more intuitively than anyone ever had, or likely ever will again.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230509142236/https://www.theatlantic.com/culture/archive/2022/09/queen-elizabeth-ii-death-british-monarchy-identity/671392/|archivedate=9 May 2023}}
## {{lb|en|intransitive}} To behave in ways that carry meaning in social contexts.
##: {{ux|en|Individuals in societies '''perform''' all the time.}}

