===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|knobbe}}, from {{der|en|gml|knobbe|t=knob, knot in wood, bud}}, probably ultimately from a variant of {{der|en|gem-pro|*knappô||knob, lump}}, one of several ''kn-'' words related to {{m|gem-pro|*knudaną|t=to knead}}.

Compare {{cog|dum|knobbe}}, {{m|dum|cnoppe}}, {{cog|gml|knobbe||knob, knot in wood}}, {{m|gml|knubbe}}, {{cog|nl|knop||knob, button, bud}}, {{cog|ofs|knopp}}, {{m|ofs|knapp}}, {{cog|goh|knopf||bud, pommel of a sword, knot, loop}}, {{cog|gmh|knospe}}, {{cog|de|Knopf||button, knob}}, {{m|de|Knospe||bud}}, {{cog|da|knap||button}}, {{m|da|knop||knob, button, bud}}, {{cog|fo|knappur||button}}, {{cog|is|hnappur||button}}, {{cog|nrn|knapp||round button}}, {{m|nrn|knubbi||potato}}, {{m|nrn|snapp||knob, round top}}, {{cog|no|-}} and {{cog|sv|knapp||button}}, {{cog|no|knopp||bud}}, {{cog|sv|knopp||bud, knob}}, {{cog|fi|nappi||button, knob}}, {{m|fi|nuppi||knob, pommel}}, {{cog|non|knyfill|t=short horn}}.

Also compare {{cog|enm|knap}}, {{m|enm|knappe||small projection, knob (button, tassel, tuft, etc.), hill, hilltop, etc.)}}, from {{inh|en|ang|cnæp}}, {{m|ang|cnæpp||summit, top}}, which is possibly related to {{der|en|non|knappr|t=small projection, knob (button, head of a stick, etc.)}} (whence {{cog|en|knop}}), from {{der|en|gem-pro|*knappô||knob, lump}}, {{m|gem-pro|*knuppô||lump, clod}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|knob}}, {{m|nl|knobbel|t=knob}}, {{cog|de|Knubbe}}, {{m|de|Knubbel|t=knob}}. See also {{m|en|knop}}. For the sense of a rounded hill, see {{m|en|nab}} and {{m|en|knab}}.

